scope two roots behind one cadence choir; dated_path surfaced as a gate rather than touched; the granary twins and 27 MALA lines left named
obs The send cost two rebases and three renumbers, and the derived spine was right each time. I booked against a spine read after a fetch, and a peer published %612 at 20260908.052546 while I worked -- later than my 052314, yet PUBLISHED, so rule 3 beat the earlier stamp and mine moved. Then %613 went the same way on the second rebase. The rows are cited by stamp everywhere but the shelf filename, which is the only reason three renumbers cost four files rather than twenty.
obs The card is the scarcer surface. Upstream's ITINERARY stood 51 bytes from its bound, so my account was cut from 2,475 bytes to 706 across seven passes -- the shelf and this log carry what the card cannot. A per-ship block of 2,048 is the bound's own arithmetic and there is no room for it at eight ships.
status GREEN before the send -- e116, e117, e118 and e133 scans exit 0, equinox_e133 witness green, season_leaf_choir 33 of 33 red=0. dated_path stays RED and gated on Keaton's word; it is %568's open finding, not a lap's to move.
recommend keep-going -- granary 0x66 and 0x69 carry %608's exact shape, a byte flip printed as a lie over a seed written in the source
